<div class="min-h-screen bg-base-200/40">
  <.site_header current_scope={@current_scope} active={:ai} />

  <!-- hero -->
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6 pt-16 pb-10">
    <div class="max-w-3xl">
      <h1 class="badge badge-primary badge-outline mb-4">AI Analytics</h1>
      <h2 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-semibold tracking-tight leading-tight">
        <code class="font-mono">llms.txt</code> Analytics — Free AI Data Metrics
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-lg text-base-content/70 leading-relaxed">
        You shipped an agent, an MCP server, a CLI, a VS Code extension. You have no idea
        whether anyone runs it twice. Fetch one URL with your account ID and you have usage
        analytics — no SDK to install, no API key to exchange, no JSON to assemble, nothing to
        add to your dependency tree.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-8 rounded-box bg-base-100 border border-base-300 p-5 sm:p-6">
      <.agent_preview />
      <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <.link navigate={~p"/dashboard"} class="btn btn-primary btn-sm">See it in the dashboard</.link>
        <a href={~p"/llms.txt"} class="btn btn-outline btn-sm font-mono text-xs">
          Read llms.txt
        </a>
        <span class="text-sm text-base-content/50">
          Hand it to your coding agent. It reads the rest.
        </span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- the API -->
  <section id="ping" class="border-y border-base-300 bg-base-100">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6 py-16">
      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Every parameter there is</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base-content/70 max-w-2xl">
        Four you will use, a few you might, and anything else you add is kept as an attribute on
        the event. There is no schema to extend.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-8 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="table table-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Parameter</th>
              <th>Meaning</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr :for={
              row <- [
                {"uid", "Your account ID."},
                {"c · cc · s_p · n",
                 "Your user's city, county, state/province and nation. Required — see below."},
                {"event",
                 "What happened — page_view, tool_called, run_completed. Anything you like."},
                {"project",
                 "Which of your tools this is. Keeps them separate under one account."},
                {"type", "Channel. ai for an AI tool; that is the default here."},
                {"sid",
                 "Session ID. Pings that share one are grouped into a run or conversation."},
                {"path", "A screen or step. With event=page_view it becomes a real pageview."},
                {"…anything", "Kept as an attribute: &tool=search&latency_ms=420&outcome=success"}
              ]
            }>
              <td class="font-mono text-xs whitespace-nowrap">
                <span :if={elem(row, 0) == "…anything"} class="font-bold">
                  <span class="wa-ellipsis" aria-hidden="true"><i class="not-italic">.</i><i class="not-italic">.</i><i class="not-italic">.</i></span>anything
                </span>
                <span :if={elem(row, 0) != "…anything"}>{elem(row, 0)}</span>
              </td>
              <td class="text-sm text-base-content/70">{elem(row, 1)}</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <div class="rounded-box border border-base-300 p-5">
            <div class="text-sm font-medium">
              Sessions and flow, for free
            </div>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-base-content/60">
              Consecutive pings are grouped into a session automatically, and the server numbers
              your pageviews and links each to the one before it. Send
              <code class="font-mono text-xs">event=page_view&amp;path=/chat</code>
              each time — you never send a sequence number — and you get a flow graph showing
              which paths lead where and where runs stop. Pass
              <code class="font-mono text-xs">sid</code>
              when you want exact control.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-box border border-base-300 p-5">
            <div class="text-sm font-medium">
              <code class="font-mono text-xs">event=page_view</code> is special
            </div>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-base-content/60">
              With a <code class="font-mono text-xs">path</code>, it is recorded as a real
              pageview rather than a generic event — so it lands in the pages report and the
              page-to-page flow graph.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-box border border-base-300 p-5">
            <div class="text-sm font-medium">You send the location; we don't guess it</div>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-base-content/60">
              <code class="font-mono text-xs">c</code>, <code class="font-mono text-xs">cc</code>,
              <code class="font-mono text-xs">s_p</code>
              and <code class="font-mono text-xs">n</code>
              carry your user's city, county, state/province and nation. A ping arrives from
              wherever your tool runs — a container, a serverless region three countries away —
              so its address says where the software is, not where the person is. Guessing from
              it would give a confident, wrong answer.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-box border border-base-300 p-5">
            <div class="text-sm font-medium">Your account ID is the whole credential</div>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-base-content/60">
              It identifies an account and grants nothing else. Unknown IDs get the same empty 204
              as real ones, so the endpoint cannot be used to test whether an account exists.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- what to log -->
  <section id="events" class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6 py-16">
    <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What's worth logging</h3>
    <p class="mt-3 text-base-content/70 max-w-2xl">
      Event names are yours to choose. These are the ones that answer the questions you
      actually have about a tool you just shipped.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-8 grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
      <div
        :for={
          item <- [
            {"install · first_run · upgraded",
             "Is anyone adopting it, and are they staying current? Pass version=."},
            {"run_started · run_completed",
             "How many runs finish. Pass outcome=success|error|cancelled."},
            {"tool_called", "Which capabilities actually get used. Pass tool= and latency_ms=."},
            {"page_view", "Which screens or steps people reach, with path=."},
            {"error", "What breaks in the wild. Pass kind= — never the message text."},
            {"feedback", "Whether it helped. Pass rating=up|down."}
          ]
        }
        class="rounded-box bg-base-100 border border-base-300 p-5"
      >
        <div class="text-sm font-mono">{elem(item, 0)}</div>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-base-content/60">{elem(item, 1)}</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- the integration, as the conversation it is -->
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6 py-16">
    <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">This is the entire integration</h3>
    <p class="mt-3 text-base-content/70 max-w-2xl mb-8">
      Hand one line to your coding agent. It reads the contract, opens an account, wires up the
      events, and updates your own llms.txt so the next agent does the same.
    </p>
    <.integration_chat base_url={@base_url} />
  </section>

  <!-- cta -->
  <section class="border-t border-base-300 bg-base-100">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6 py-16 text-center">
      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Integrate with AI, now!</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base-content/70 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
        Copy this and give it to whatever coding agent works on your project. It does the rest —
        reads the contract, opens an account, reports the events, and updates your own
        <code class="font-mono text-sm">llms.txt</code>
        so the next agent does the same.
      </p>

      <pre class="mt-6 mx-auto max-w-3xl text-left bg-base-200 rounded-box p-5 text-sm whitespace-pre-wrap break-words"><code>{agent_prompt(@base_url)}</code></pre>

      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3 justify-center">
        <.cta current_scope={@current_scope} />
        <a href={~p"/llms.txt"} class="btn btn-outline font-mono text-sm">Read llms.txt</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <.site_footer />
</div>
